#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198079 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9f0da82-f79b-5056-ac99-b401eb1630c7" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Rectangular Sets Using Set-Builder Notation"}] :question/id #uuid "06ee94f5-8d8c-537b-83ff-69d6e351e4d4" :question/math-academy-id "e-13145" :question/problem "Describe the set of points shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4392/Source/Images/9ec63ab02935633fe11a069f72b4098b.png)" :question/worked-solution "Since all points $P(x,y)$ in our set lie at integer values of $x$ and $y$, we have $x\\in \\mathbb Z, y\\in\\mathbb Z$, and therefore\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb Z\\times\\mathbb Z = \\mathbb Z^2\n$$\n\nThe points $P(x,y)$ satisfy the condition\n\n$$\nx \\in \\{1,2,3\\}, \\quad y \\in \\{-3,-2,-1,0,1,2 \\}\n$$\n\nIn other words,\n\n$$\n1 \\leq x \\leq 3, \\quad -3 \\leq y \\leq 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\n\\Big\\{(x,y) \\in \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\mathbb Z^2} \\:: \\: \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1} \\leq x \\leq \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}3}, \\: \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}-3} \\leq y \\leq \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2} \\Big\\}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198080 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a3df69f-653e-5148-bd46-580097e43362" :knowledge-point/title "Linear Boundaries, Two Conditions"}] :question/id #uuid "487a42f5-a562-5df1-9225-dbbc0046c002" :question/math-academy-id "e-13156" :question/problem "Describe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4392/Source/Images/7cac8ef29134e3864a40c2aac8a76339.png)" :question/worked-solution "The points in our set include all those that lie *above* the line $y=2x$ and *below* the line $y=3$.\n\nA point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\ny \\gt 2x, \\qquad y < 3\n$$\n\nCombining this into a single condition, we have\n\n$$\n2x \\lt y < 3\n$$\n\nWe must also include the line $y= 3$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the corresponding inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\n2x \\lt y \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\leq} 3\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in \\mathbb R, y\\in\\mathbb R$, and therefore\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R = \\mathbb R^2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\Big\\{(x,y)\\in \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\mathbb R^2} \\:: \\: 2x \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\lt} y \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\leq} 3 \\Big\\}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198081 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36fa0f2f-3812-521a-aca3-900556be84ff" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Non-Rectangular Sets Using Set-Builder Notation"}] :question/id #uuid "88fba9fd-d05b-546c-89aa-edcf8d2fc93e" :question/math-academy-id "e-13158" :question/problem "Use set builder notation to describe the shaded region given in the diagram below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4392/Source/Images/fecea6c55850b5b4d4d702f2d38d5315.png)" :question/worked-solution "The equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}=1\n$$\n\nHere, the elliptical boundary of our region has horizontal radius $a=5$ and vertical radius $b=4$. Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x^{2}}{25} + \\frac{y^{2}}{16} &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as\n\n$$\n16x^2 + 25y^2 = 400\n$$\n\nWe're interested in describing all points that lie *outside* the ellipse. So, let's pick any point outside the region, say, $(10,0)$. Substituting its coordinates into the equation of the boundary, we obtain\n\n$$\n16(10)^2+25(0)^2 \\: {\\color{blue}>} \\: 400\n$$\n\nAs a result, the points $P(x,y)$ in our region are any real numbers that satisfy the condition\n\n$$\n16x^2 + 25y^2 \\gt 400\n$$\n\nWe must also include the curve\n\n$$\n16x^2 + 25y^2 = 400\n$$\n\nsince the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\n16x^2 + 25y^2 \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\geq} 400\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\Big\\{(x,y) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2 \\:: \\: \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}16}x^2 + \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}25}y^2 \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\geq} 400 \\bigg\\}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198091 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e694b201-5ec6-5cc3-a608-2447d1cc1ed3" :knowledge-point/title "Linear Boundaries, One Condition"}] :question/id #uuid "56561d78-d192-5197-81f7-303abf75018f" :question/math-academy-id "e-13183" :question/problem "Describe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4392/Source/Images/592f96a01b0588aaacaa43f58d33b785.png)" :question/worked-solution "Since all points $P(x,y)$ in our set lie at integer values of $x$ and $y$, we have $x\\in \\mathbb Z, y\\in\\mathbb Z$, and therefore\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb Z\\times\\mathbb Z = \\mathbb Z^2\n$$\n\nThe points in our set include all those that lie *below* the line\n\n$$\ny=x+2\n$$\n\nFor those points, the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\ny \\lt x+2\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that some points in our set lie *on* the line. We include these points by changing the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\ny \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\leq}x+2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as follows:\n\n$$\n\\Big\\{(x,y) \\in \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\mathbb Z ^2} \\:: \\: y \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\leq}x+2 \\Big\\}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198699 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9f0da82-f79b-5056-ac99-b401eb1630c7" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Rectangular Sets Using Set-Builder Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "821e9945-6e26-5748-afa0-f57b96186e8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195145} {:answer/id #uuid "b5270c9d-d1c4-5067-b936-eec91be66119" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195146} {:answer/id #uuid "e8cf67e0-5ab8-54b4-89e9-e3af0dd0bed7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195147} {:answer/id #uuid "28f661bd-24e7-5e72-9eb4-dcb664c782bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195148}] :answer-field/id #uuid "1d69d30e-7173-557e-b39e-1055c2b3b1a7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195144} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8363ed9d-3217-5084-8b1f-48c8dc58b3bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195150} {:answer/id #uuid "8fffd2b8-f4b8-5855-80d5-b7c55a134127" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195151} {:answer/id #uuid "9a3b87f3-4cff-56c4-b4b5-4fa276f4054c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195152} {:answer/id #uuid "f6f796d7-3c75-5f2b-a0b2-98dad40efee5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195153}] :answer-field/id #uuid "c1458b5b-a2c5-5d69-9c23-40831104cd33" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195149} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a69cfec3-34de-5197-a34c-4bf3b78fb6d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186195155} {:answer/id #uuid "a89ebb94-637e-5281-a520-28a2f2bace86" :answer/type {:db/id